# Live since 2026-08-26.
#
# The executable carries whatever label file_contexts gives it, and that label
# is baked into the image as an xattr, so labelling it only works when this
# policy ships in the same transaction. init refuses to start a service whose
# executable carries a type the loaded policy does not define, and Permissive
# does not change that: it governs whether denials block, not whether a type
# exists. That is why the label was dropped when only system could be written.
#
# The transaction now writes system_ext, and system_ext is rebuilt with GApps
# rather than preserved, so the policy and the label ship together.
type odinlightsd, domain, coredomain;
type odinlightsd_exec, system_file_type, exec_type, file_type;

init_daemon_domain(odinlightsd)

get_prop(odinlightsd, build_prop)
unix_socket_send(odinlightsd, logdw, logd)

# No rule for the LED nodes here on purpose. They carry a vendor label, and the
# stock lights HAL that also writes them runs as hal_light_default, which
# system_ext policy cannot name: checkpolicy rejects it as an unknown type.
# Relabelling the nodes into a type this policy can grant would therefore take
# the access away from the stock HAL with no way to give it back, and the LED
# would stop working entirely the moment anything runs Enforcing.
#
# The daemon works today because the device runs Permissive. Granting it
# properly belongs with the Qualcomm vendor policy work, in a policy unit where
# both hal_light_default and the vendor sysfs type are visible.

# Traversing /sys/class/leds to reach the LED nodes. sysfs_leds is a public
# plat type, so unlike the brightness files themselves this one is ours to
# grant, and it was 326 of this daemon's denials. It does not make the daemon
# work under Enforcing on its own: the brightness files below it keep
# vendor_sysfs_graphics, which a coredomain cannot be granted from here.
allow odinlightsd sysfs_leds:dir search;
